<!-- §01 ─ The AR Blindspot -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Autoregressive Blindspot</div>
<h2>Why Left-to-Right Is a Constraint, Not a Requirement</h2>
<p>Every mainstream LLM — GPT-4, Llama 3, Qwen2.5 — generates text the same way: left-to-right, one token at a time. Each token is conditioned only on what came before. This <em>autoregressive</em> paradigm has proven powerful, but it has a structural blindspot: the model can never revise an early token based on a later one.</p>
<p>Consider the sentence "The <strong>capital</strong> of France is Paris." When an AR model writes "The capital", it has already committed — it cannot change "capital" even if downstream tokens would suggest a better word. Humans don't write this way; we often start a sentence knowing roughly how it ends, or revise early words after writing later ones.</p>
<p>What if a model could see the full context — past <em>and</em> future — when deciding every token? That's exactly what masked diffusion enables. Instead of predicting left-to-right, the model starts with all tokens masked and iteratively unmasks them, using <strong>fully bidirectional attention</strong> at each step.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">AR vs. Masked Diffusion — generation comparison</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Play to animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding:.875rem 1.25rem 1.25rem">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-bottom:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <button class="mode-btn active" id="btn-play-gen" onclick="playGen()">▶ Play</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" id="btn-reset-gen" onclick="resetGen()">↺ Reset</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="gen-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;border-radius:8px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-top:.5rem">
      <span style="font-size:.67rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">← Autoregressive (causal)</span>
      <span style="font-size:.67rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Masked Diffusion (bidirectional) →</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The visualization above shows the same sentence being generated two ways. On the left, AR reveals tokens one by one from left to right — each committed forever. On the right, masked diffusion starts with all tokens hidden and reveals the highest-confidence ones first, bidirectionally, until the sentence is complete.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 ─ Masked Diffusion -->
<section id="s-masking" data-code="masking">
<div class="section-no">02 — Masked Diffusion</div>
<h2>Corrupting and Restoring: How Diffusion Works for Text</h2>
<p>Masked diffusion for language works like a principled game of fill-in-the-blank. During training, a clean sentence is corrupted by randomly masking tokens according to a masking ratio <strong>t ∈ [0, 1]</strong>: at t=0, no tokens are masked; at t=1, every token is masked. The model learns to predict all masked tokens simultaneously given all visible ones.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Masking ratio t — token corruption level</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">adjust t to see more/fewer masked tokens</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:.875rem" id="t-btns">
      <button class="mode-btn active" onclick="setT(0,'t0')">t = 0.0</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" onclick="setT(0.25,'t025')">t = 0.25</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" onclick="setT(0.5,'t05')">t = 0.50</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" onclick="setT(0.75,'t075')">t = 0.75</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" onclick="setT(1.0,'t1')">t = 1.0</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="mask-canvas" style="width:100%;height:160px;display:block;border-radius:8px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <p style="margin:.5rem 0 0;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Training objective: predict all <span style="color:var(--amber)">[MASK]</span> tokens from visible context — using bidirectional attention across the full sequence.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The training loss is a re-weighted negative log-likelihood over masked positions:</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Masked Diffusion Objective (Eq. 1)</div>
  <span class="math-var">L</span>(θ) = −E<sub>t,x₀,xₜ</sub> [<sup>1</sup>/<sub>t</sub> Σᵢ <b>1</b>[xₜⁱ = <span style="color:var(--amber)">MASK</span>] · log p<sub>θ</sub>(x₀ⁱ | xₜ)]
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">// only masked positions contribute to the loss; t controls the masking density</span>
</div>

<p>This differs from BERT's masked language modeling: the masking ratio t is sampled uniformly from [0,1] at each step, not fixed at 15%. This trains the model to denoise at every corruption level simultaneously — the key property that makes generation possible at inference time.</p>
<p>The architecture uses <strong>fully bidirectional (non-causal) attention</strong> — every token can attend to every other token at every position. This is fundamentally different from GPT-style causal masking, which blocks future tokens. Bidirectionality means richer representations, especially for tasks requiring global context like math and code.</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 ─ Architecture -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">03 — iLLaDA Architecture</div>
<h2>What iLLaDA Changed from LLaDA</h2>
<p>LLaDA was the first proof that a large masked diffusion model could match AR models in instruction-following. But it only trained on 2.3T tokens and averaged 51.1% across benchmarks — substantially behind Qwen2.5 7B at 63.3%. iLLaDA's goal was to close that gap through careful engineering of the training recipe.</p>

<div class="vp">
