<p>The most impactful change is scale: 12T pre-training tokens vs 2.3T. But the architectural tweaks matter too. <strong>Grouped-Query Attention (GQA)</strong> reduces the KV-cache memory footprint — important because diffusion models benefit from KV-cache-like mechanisms for efficiency. <strong>Tied embeddings</strong> reduce the parameter count without hurting performance, freeing capacity for more layers/depth at fixed parameter budget.</p>
<p>The SFT format change is subtle but significant: prior LLaDA-style training only masked response tokens, never prompt tokens. iLLaDA trains on full-sequence random masks (prompt + response together), matching the pre-training objective exactly. This removes the training-inference mismatch and, combined with multi-epoch training, produces substantially stronger instruction-following.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 ─ Confidence Scoring -->
<section id="s-scoring" data-code="scoring">
<div class="section-no">04 — Confidence-Based Scoring</div>
<h2>A Better Way to Evaluate Diffusion Models on Multiple-Choice</h2>
<p>Many standard benchmarks — HellaSwag, ARC-Challenge, PIQA — are multiple-choice: given a question, pick the best answer from 4 options. For AR models, scoring is simple: compute the log-probability of each candidate and pick the highest. For diffusion models, this requires estimating an <em>upper bound</em> on the negative log-likelihood, which turns out to be a suboptimal evaluator.</p>
<p>iLLaDA introduces <strong>confidence-based scoring</strong>: instead of estimating likelihood, the model is run interactively to score each candidate. Starting from an all-masked candidate, tokens are revealed one-at-a-time in the order the model is most confident about:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Confidence scoring vs likelihood — step-by-step</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Next to step through scoring</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="margin-bottom:.75rem;padding:.75rem 1rem;border-radius:8px;background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border)">
      <div style="font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Question: "The capital of France is ___"</div>
      <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <span style="font-size:.78rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--text2)">A: <span style="color:var(--cyan)">Paris</span></span>
        <span style="font-size:.78rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--text2)">B: <span style="color:var(--muted)">London</span></span>
        <span style="font-size:.78rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--text2)">C: <span style="color:var(--muted)">Berlin</span></span>
        <span style="font-size:.78rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--text2)">D: <span style="color:var(--muted)">Rome</span></span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div id="conf-steps" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.5rem;margin-bottom:.875rem"></div>
    <div class="step-nav" style="margin-top:0">
      <button id="conf-prev" onclick="confStep(-1)">← Prev</button>
      <button id="conf-next" onclick="confStep(1)">Next →</button>
      <span id="conf-prog" class="step-prog"></span>
    </div>
    <div style="margin-top:.875rem;padding:.625rem .875rem;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2);border:1px solid var(--border)">
      <div style="font-size:.65rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.07em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.4rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Score comparison — ARC-Challenge</div>
      <div style="display:flex;gap:2rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <span style="font-size:.78rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Likelihood: <span style="color:var(--muted2)">60.2%</span></span>
        <span style="font-size:.78rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Confidence: <span style="color:var(--green);font-weight:700">60.8% (+0.6)</span></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The confidence score for candidate y is:</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Confidence Scoring (Eq. 2)</div>
  <span class="math-var">i<sub>k</sub></span> = argmax<sub>i∈M<sub>k−1</sub></sub> p<sub>θ</sub>(yⁱ | p, ỹ<sub>k−1</sub>)&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">// pick highest-confidence masked token</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-var">S<sub>conf</sub></span>(y|p) = Σ<sub>k=1</sub><sup>L</sup> log p<sub>θ</sub>(y<sup>i<sub>k</sub></sup> | p, ỹ<sub>k−1</sub>)&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">// sum log-probs in reveal order</span>
</div>
<p>This is not a likelihood estimate — it's a task-specific scoring surrogate. By always revealing the token the model is most sure about first, the score accumulates evidence in the most-informative order. The result: +2.3 on HellaSwag, +1.3 on PIQA, +0.6 on ARC-Challenge vs likelihood scoring.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 ─ Block Generation -->
<section id="s-gen" data-code="gen">
<div class="section-no">05 — Variable-Length Block Generation</div>
<h2>Generating Open-Ended Text with a Diffusion Model</h2>
<p>Masked diffusion naturally handles fixed-length sequences: mask everything, run the sampler, done. But real language generation requires <em>variable-length</em> output — a question might need 20 words or 2000. iLLaDA solves this with <strong>variable-length block generation</strong>: an iterative process that appends blocks of masks and decodes them one block at a time.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Block generation — step navigator</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Next to walk through generation</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="gen-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Append a mask block</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Append a fixed block of [MASK] tokens (e.g. 32) after the prompt. The block size is a hyperparameter — smaller blocks = more AR-like; larger = more parallel.</div>
          <div class="step-code">sequence = prompt + [MASK] × block_size</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Model forward pass — predict all masks</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Run the bidirectional model on the full sequence. Every masked position gets a probability distribution over the vocabulary — computed simultaneously with full attention to all visible tokens.</div>
          <div class="step-code">logits = model(sequence)  # bidirectional</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Reveal high-confidence tokens</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Rank all masked positions by max softmax probability. Reveal the top-k% as their predicted tokens — these become "visible" for subsequent steps.</div>
          <div class="step-code">conf = max(softmax(logits[mask_pos]))
top_k = argsort(conf)[-reveal_k:]
sequence[top_k] = predicted[top_k]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Keep low-confidence tokens masked</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Low-confidence positions stay masked. The model will reconsider them in the next diffusion step, now with more context from newly-revealed tokens (low-confidence remasking strategy from MaskGIT).</div>
          <div class="step-code">bottom_conf = argsort(conf)[:mask_k]
sequence[bottom_conf] = [MASK]  # remask</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Check [EOS] — or append another block</div>
          <div class="step-desc">If the decoded block contains [EOS] or a stop token, generation terminates. Otherwise a new block of [MASK] tokens is appended and the process repeats until the max budget is reached.</div>
          <div class="step-code">if EOS in decoded_block: stop
else: sequence += [MASK] × block_size</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="gen-prev" onclick="genStep(-1)">← Prev</button>
      <button id="gen-next" onclick="genStep(1)">Next →</button>
      <span id="gen-prog" class="step-prog">Step 1 of 5</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Block size controls the quality-speed tradeoff. For most benchmarks, iLLaDA uses block size 32 with max generation 1024 tokens. For HumanEval (code), full block size = 512 is used since semi-autoregressive blocks hurt performance on structured code. For MMLU (single letter answer), block size = 3 suffices.</p>
<p>A key discovery from the SFT experiments: iLLaDA keeps <em>improving</em> with more fine-tuning epochs. Training on the same 25B-token SFT corpus for 12 epochs (vs the usual 1) produces measurably better results — especially on reasoning-heavy benchmarks like MATH and MMLU-Pro. This is consistent with findings that diffusion LMs are "super data learners" — they can extract more signal from repeated exposure than AR models.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 ─ Results -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>Competitive with Qwen2.5 7B — Without a Single Autoregressive Token</h2>
<p>The key question was whether fully bidirectional diffusion training at scale could match autoregressive baselines. Across 8 benchmarks spanning general language, math, and code, iLLaDA achieves a <strong>63.9% average</strong> on base model evaluation — edging out Qwen2.5 7B (63.3%) and dramatically outperforming the previous diffusion champion LLaDA (51.1%).</p>

